A||N||8||jhogans||Pointers on Washing Engine???||||1||
Z||000000||jhogans||08-21-2000||12:08 AM||||I plan to wash my engine on under the hood.  I need to know what stuff should I use to clean the engine and what do I need to look out for when washing the engine.<P>Thanks for any info.l||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||08-21-2000||12:15 AM||||When I washed my engine, I just bought some $.79 stuff from Wal Mart.  First, I covered all the electrical stuff with aluminum foil.  This is easier to use than plastic wrap; it's easier to wrap around and stays where it's put.  I foiled all the stuff that doesn't look waterproof.  Like, the battery, distributer, coil, etc.  Just spray the stuff on pretty thick.  Let it sit for about 15 minutes.  Then, wash it off with a garden hose (I guess you could also use a pressure washer; just don't let the aluminum foil come off.) Let it dry for about 15 to 20 minutes before starting.  By the way, it's easier to wash when the engine is either cool or slightly warm. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||08-21-2000||12:20 AM||||I usually bring it to the self-serve car wash and use the pressure hose.  I do it first thing in the morning when the engine is still cool.  I leave it running while I hose it down....this way I know when I've hit something that I shouldn't have (like the coil or distributor).<P>||||reg||6
Z||000003||Bugsy||08-21-2000||05:44 AM||||I used to be shop manager of an auto detail shop, Before spraying, I use a degreaser called SolvsIt, mixed 1 part solvsit, to 4 parts water. I spray the engine down with this mix, and let it stand fot aprox 10 minutes, then use the pressure washer. <P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||08-21-2000||10:37 AM||||I use "Castrol Super Clean". It is like All Purpose cleaner. It cleans the grease off good and dose not stink like spray degreaser!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000005||Raydar||08-21-2000||02:00 PM||||Try using "Mean Green". The only place I've ever seen it is Dollar General stores.<BR>Works even better than Simple Green, IMHO. Avoid the obvious electrical stuff.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>When I die, I want to go peacefully, in my sleep, like Grandpa.<BR>Not screaming, like the passengers in his car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-21-2000||04:32 PM||||Avoid aiming pressure washers directly at seals. You can blow water into the engine/tranny or even ruin the seal. I'm talking mainly about axle/crank type seals. Also avoid the oil cap and PCV hardware for the same reason.<P>Even then don't get to close to things with the end of the wand. You'd be suprised the amount of damage you can do.<P>Unless your sparkplug wires are in really good shape expect to take them off and dry everything. <P>Take tools so you can get the distributor cap off and dry out under there. You almost always get water under the cap if you hit it. It seems to be a car wash magnet. No matter how hard you try it's almost imposible not to hit it at least once with the pressure washer.||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||08-21-2000||10:38 PM||||The best cleaner I've used lately is Orange Cleaner by zek from HOME DEPOT. You can use it straight or mix it with H2O. It seems to cut anything. Spray on, let sit 10 mins, hose off. Repete for thick crap.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||jhogans||08-22-2000||11:47 AM||||Thanks for all of the info guys.  This is very helpful||||reg||1
